WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.110
<v Examiner>Four marks.

2
00:00:02.110 --> 00:00:04.656
<v Examiner>Explain the purpose of health screening.

3
00:00:04.656 --> 00:00:07.774
<v Examiner>Give one organised and one opportunistic example.

4
00:00:07.774 --> 00:00:12.738
<v Examiner>Then discuss why some people don't take part, and what nurses can do about it.

5
00:00:13.188 --> 00:00:18.745
The purpose: screening identifies people who are at risk, or who already have a disease,

6
00:00:18.745 --> 00:00:20.386
before they have symptoms.

7
00:00:20.386 --> 00:00:25.753
Finding it early reduces the chance of developing the condition, or of dying from it.

8
00:00:26.203 --> 00:00:31.310
Organised programmes are funded and monitored by the National Screening Unit.

9
00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:36.682
There are six: antenatal, newborn metabolic, universal newborn hearing, cervical,

10
00:00:36.682 --> 00:00:37.810
breast and bowel.

11
00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:42.983
Newborn metabolic screening is a heel-prick blood test a few days after birth.

12
00:00:42.983 --> 00:00:47.294
Free breast screening is for women aged forty-five to sixty-nine,

13
00:00:47.294 --> 00:00:49.151
a mammogram every two years.

14
00:00:49.151 --> 00:00:54.855
Opportunistic screening isn't run by the unit: you offer it if the person is eligible,

15
00:00:54.855 --> 00:00:55.651
or they ask.

16
00:00:55.651 --> 00:01:00.559
Examples: S T I checks, prostate checks for older men, and blood pressure.

17
00:01:01.009 --> 00:01:03.680
Now discuss, the part with the most marks.

18
00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:06.161
Not taking part is not always a choice.

19
00:01:06.161 --> 00:01:11.568
The barriers Lisa listed: cost, distance and transport, not knowing what will happen,

20
00:01:11.568 --> 00:01:13.477
and resources only in English.

21
00:01:13.477 --> 00:01:18.820
What nurses do: take screening to people, with mobile clinics, and use self-testing,

22
00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:21.683
like home bowel kits and cervical self-swabs.

23
00:01:21.683 --> 00:01:25.245
And remember the nurse's role isn't just doing the test.

24
00:01:25.245 --> 00:01:27.663
We educate, we assess, and we support.

25
00:01:28.113 --> 00:01:31.590
One fact for the multi-choice, from her sample question.

26
00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:35.501
A false negative means the disease is present but not detected.

27
00:01:35.501 --> 00:01:38.233
That's more dangerous than a false positive,

28
00:01:38.233 --> 00:01:41.958
because the person who has the condition is told they don't.

29
00:01:42.408 --> 00:01:47.706
Screening finds risk or disease before symptoms, to reduce illness and death.

30
00:01:47.706 --> 00:01:53.349
Organised, by the National Screening Unit: antenatal, newborn heel prick, hearing,

31
00:01:53.349 --> 00:01:58.166
cervical, breast from forty-five to sixty-nine every two years, bowel.

32
00:01:58.166 --> 00:02:01.400
Opportunistic: S T I, prostate, blood pressure.

33
00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:06.286
Barriers: cost, transport, fear of the unknown, English-only resources.

34
00:02:06.286 --> 00:02:08.763
Fixes: mobile clinics, self-testing.

35
00:02:08.763 --> 00:02:11.515
False negative: has it, told they don't.
